*Attached:* Email from Lewis Silkin

+*LEWIS SILKIN ADVICE BELOW:*+  

# *The answers depend on what you mean by “fully embrace” worker status on Principal Model:* {color:#006644}- to help explain the key choice I split this out below:{color}
## {color:#006644}If we were to make drivers “hourly workers” (i.e. pay them “by the hour” while online then I have no problems with a requirement that they must accept all work assigned to them – I wouldn’t even frame that as an acceptance rate but rather if we pay you for your time you must do all work we assign you during that time.  We could even have no option to reject.{color}
## {color:#006644}If we only want them to be workers while “on trip” (let’s call that “half embracing worker status”: then we cannot have acceptance rates (as they create an obligation to work “while online”) so it is very likely we will get NMW claims / holiday pay claims relating to online time for which we are not paying them.{color}
# *I’ll then answer your questions by reference to these two choices:*
## {color:#006644}minimum level of acceptance – fine on “fully embrace” not on “half embracing”.  As I say, I think you can go further on “full embrace” with no option to reject.{color}
## {color:#006644}maximum levels or rejection or no answer – fine on “fully embrace” not on “half embracing”.  As I say, I think you can go further on “full embrace” with no option to reject.{color}
## {color:#006644}certain level of courtesy & services (greetings; good bye, temperature; aircon, music) – fine on both* (these are obligations only while “on trip” so fine on either.{color}
## {color:#006644}min level of rider ratings – fine on both* (these are obligations only while “on trip” so fine on either.{color}
## {color:#006644}open/close the category at certain times of the days >> could require a minimum of OH during night time and week-ends – fine on “fully embrace” not on “half embracing”.  I have understood this as essentially being a “shift booking” type system – where to work you have to book a “shift” which might be 3 / 5 hours and we might have min shifts required per week to book etc*.{color}
## {color:#006644}and/or change prices depending on the peak/off peak – I am not sure I fully understand this but if it just about us changing the price we pay between peak / off peak (don’t we do that anyway by surge?), then fine for us to do as long as we always pay at least NMW (inc expenses), holiday and pensions etc.{color}
# *Two notes of caution, though:*
## {color:#006644}I have used an * on two points above.  In principle these are fine for workers but remember that we will still want to argue that these drivers are “only” workers and not “PAYE employees” – so we move away from the simple “worker test” (can you sub / are you a business) into the “multi-factorial” test for employment status (looking at a range of factors are you, on balance, an employee?).  These two are potentially fine but we would want to look at the overall model carefully to test whether we think we are getting too close to employment.{color}
## {color:#006644}Remember the potential consequences of Labour Govt.  I have sent you an email on their immediate plans (Employment Bill to be announced tomorrow – that will not contain “single status of worker” proposals yet – and those may still be killed) but whatever we build we should be alive to the fact that the goal posts may shift with those changes and all “workers” could get employment rights soon.{color}

 *One final point which may be relevant – not only* +*can*+ *we do certain things above (as indicated) we* +*should*+ *do at least some.  If we are to have a split fleet, we should have clear differences between the two (how they work, min expectations etc) to justify the different statuses.*

